YAAP: 64-cdi-mon-hum-mal-law ... giving it a good, swift kick
****

As you can probably guess from the 64 in the subject, my ascension
streak continues.

I originally wanted to do a vegan, weaponless, genocideless, poly-less
game.  I ate something I shouldn't have (a pudding, I think), giving
me vegetarian instead of vegan.  Genocideless went out the window when
a throne asked what I wanted to genocide (gremlins).  I wound up wound
up with vegetarian, weaponless, and poly-less in the final set of
conducts.

The early game was more interesting than usual.  A Dlvl.02 shop had +0
jumping boots, and a +1 T-shirt was found on Dlvl.03.  There were also
shops on levels 04 and 05.  By the time I reached sokoban, I was
reasonably well kitted-out.  Getting an early amulet of ESP was a big
help, since floating eyes weren't on the menu.  Sokoban had an amulet
of reflection, which was very good.  In Minetown, Izchak had an
uncursed magic lamp.  The temple was cross-aligned, which was bad.

Just below minetown, I came across a bones pile with polytrapped
horrors.  Foood got grey dragoned with two magic lamps.  Fortunately,
team conflict took care of much of the mess.  The dragon dropped
scales.  The scales went on around turn 6050.  As everyone knows, body
armour and low level monks don't get along well.  For offense, I was
restricted to either giving the monsters a good, swift kick or using
wands/spells on them.  Unfortunately, as I learned this game, there
are many tough monsters that can't be hurt by kicking.

My top priority became blessing my lamps so I could get out of the
dragon scales.  I didn't find either a co-aligned temple or an
unattended altar until Dlvl.15 (somewhere around turn 15,000).  With
my surplus of non-blessed magic lamps taken care of, it was time to
summon the Orb of Detection (half spell damage + telepathy) and a
blessed greased fireproof bag of holding.  One level down there was a
bones pile with Magicbane (bruceleroy99, by a hallucinogen-distorted
gargoyle).

While on the way to the Medusa, I wished for a Magic marker and got my
AC down to -31.  I killed Medusa by invoking the Orb of Detection to
toggle invisibility.

The castle was cleared without too much trouble.  There was a peaceful
golden naga in the throne room.  I didn't want to use conflict around
it, so clearing the barracks involved getting inside, closing (and
locking) the door, then inviting team conflict to the party.  I kicked
a lieutenant off one of the doorways before claiming it for myself.

While in the Valley of the Dead, Tenaya (smile, wave!) mailed me.  I
was using a stethoscope to identify the enchantment of a bunch of
rings of increase damage.  I forgot about blessed glove damage on
demons/undead, so I had to do it twice.  I didn't have any scrolls of
identify, and didn't want to spend marker charges.  After clearing out
the Valley, it was time to say "hello" to the only quest nemesis that
still scares me, Master Kaen.  The original plan involved a scroll of
earth and the spell of drain life.

As you're about see, that's not how things went down.

I made my way to the downstair just above his lair.  Potion of
paralysis, check!  Potions of full healing, check!  Worn free action,
check!  Worn regeneration, check!  Spell of drain life, check!  Spell
of sleep, check!  Scrolls of earth, check!  Very fast ... quaff, now
check!

I wanted to be off the stairs (but lined up with them) and away from
the lava when I set up the fort.  I jumped diagonally down, lining
myself up with Kaen in the process.

He didn't immediately say "Hi!"  What the heck, ZAP!

"You kill Master Kaen!"

Wait, I pulled that one off?!?  I never pull that one off?!?

Gehennom didn't go as smoothly as I'm used to.  The teleporting liches
I use for locating the upstairs were unexpectedly troublesome.  I
genocided 'L' before returning to the castle.  I usually wait until
just before waking Rodney or just after reaching the sanctum,
depending on my mood.  Orcus made a nice statue.  The wielded Bell of
Opening took care of the shades (and a bunch of other monsters too).
Juiblex succumbed to a wand of digging and a punch.  Baalzebub and
Asmodeus both asked for (and got) 2gp.  I typo'd the castle level when
#naming my amulet of reflection (29 instead of 27), so finding Vlad
was an issue.

I picked up a cockatrice corpse shortly before arriving back at the
castle.  Unfortunately, it rotted away before I could wake up Rodney.
After getting the book, I teleported down to the VS, stepped
downstairs, stepped upstairs, teleported down, and landed in the
surrounding courtyard.  The secret door was on the second wall I went
to.  Once inside, I lined up the shot and zapped the Wand of Orcus.
The death ray hit the High Priest of Moloch and ... nothing.

Ok, time for plan B.

Out came the wand of wishing and "I'd like one blessed greased
cockatrice corpse please."

A few turns later and the High Priest of Moloch was a pile of rocks
and I was on my way with the Amulet of Yendor.

During the climb, I couldn't spam ^T teleportation.  When I woke
Rodney, my spell energy was 37(112), and it stayed absurdly low for
most of the run to Dlvl.01.

Shortly before reaching the planes, I screwed up and canceled 2
potions of speed (with some other stuff I meant to cancel).  My spell
energy situation throughout the climb meant I didn't have several
hundred turns of stacked "haste self" ready and waiting for the
planes.  The plane of Air had a conflicted peaceful ki-rin.
Fortunately, it didn't summon.

There wasn't any unusual/unexpected drama until I reached the plane of
water.  There, the portal to the Astral Plane was hiding under a
potion.  The potion was the same color as the water, and next to the
water.  I walked by it a few times.  I wasted many marker charges on c?
gold detection (and even resorted to the Orb of Detection once) while
trying to hone in on it.

On Astral:  I arrived on the left, Famine was on the left, I went
left.  The Lawful High Altar was left.  I love it when a plan comes
together.

Despite the mistakes, plane of water drama, and unfavorable spell
energy situation, the ascension run proper took only 1,959 turns.
This surprised me, I was expecting the turn count for this phase of
the game to be much higher.

